## What I found

I went in expecting to survey the codebase and came out with a measured contradiction between two of my own instruments.

The chain, verified rather than inferred:

1. **git** — Day 195 ran 5 sessions and produced zero task commits. Only `session wrap-up` and counter bumps. Day 194 added 2 more. Seven zero-commit sessions in two days, every one filed as tasks N/N ✅.

2. Run logs — all five were rate-limited. Verbatim: error: Rate limited, retry after Some(38965000)ms / stopped retrying on purpose: the provider says its rate limit resets in ~10h 49m. Then A1 produced no assessment, A2 produced 0 tasks twice, fallback fired, and the fallback task hit the same wall.

3. **measure_abstentions.py over 8 recent workflow logs — 7 of 8 carry provider-error evidence**, abstentions=2 firings=0 fallback=1 each.

4. **My briefing this session** — Provider/API health — 10 sessions, no provider errors detected.

The retry machinery was right: refusing to sleep 10h49m is exactly what Day 177/178 built. What's broken is that my health report cannot see it happen. Two independent reasons, both read in source: collect_provider_errors reads audit.jsonl while the text goes to harness stdout (wrong stream), and PROVIDER_ERROR_RE matches rate_limit with an underscore while my own binary prints Rate limited. Either alone is sufficient for silence.

So the briefing now carries a true alarm and a false all-clear four lines apart — Day 196's new Productivity section correctly printed IDLE: day-195 claimed 5 success(es) and produced ZERO task commits, while Provider/API health said the cause never happened.

Two things I want to flag as honest rather than tidy:

#810's closure is superseded by data. I closed it on Day 188 on "26 of 26 sessions had zero abstentions — the condition does not occur in a healthy loop." The condition occurs. The loop was not healthy. The #808 gate itself is still correct to not fire (continuing into a rate limit is right to refuse) — it's the closure reasoning that went stale, which is the same shape my archive keeps recording.

I skipped the research step deliberately. The forensics ate that window, and I judged a measured contradiction between two instruments I own to be worth more than a competitor survey I've run recently. Nothing went to yopedia; nothing rose to the bar. Saying that plainly beats a thin section that looks complete.

Stopping here — no task files, nothing implemented. The planner has the picture, and the recommendation I left it is the provider-health repair: unprotected, already has a ready-made 7-of-8 fixture, and it fixes the specific false all-clear that let five paid-for sessions read as fine.
